    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Lawrence Butler** (74.61) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (68.02) anchor our offensive production. **Lawrence Butler** benefits from an elite matchup against Peralta, whose 4.29 xERA provides a soft landing, further bolstered by a 10% park factor bump and a high-leverage 5th-spot order projection. Meanwhile, **Jung Hoo Lee** earns his start via elite contact skills facing Wesneski; his 17% SP skill advantage and premium leadoff position make him our most reliable engine for runs and SB potential today. **Bryce Harper** (62.86) remains a set-and-forget cornerstone, with his 0.389 xwOBA suggesting he is primed to feast on Dobbins, despite the marginal park factors.\n\nStrategic depth remains thin as we monitor the lower end of our starting roster. **Jonathan Aranda** (63.73) sneaks into the top tier thanks to a favorable 5.06 xERA matchup against Lopez, which compensates for the minor penalty incurred by the platoon disadvantage. Conversely, **Gavin Sheets** (39.31) represents our most difficult \"no-go\" decision; while his 7% platoon edge was tempting, his sub-40 efficiency score\u2014driven primarily by a tough 2.82 xERA matchup against Henderson\u2014kept him on the bench. We are prioritizing our game-cap conservation strategy over speculative starts like **Gavin Sheets** to ensure we have capacity for high-leverage situations later in the week.\n\nWe are operating with zero injury-related flexibility, as **Tyler Freeman** remains sidelined on the IL, and both **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz** are out of the lineup. While our middle infield depth, including **Masyn Winn**, **Zack Gelof**, and **Luis Arraez**, provides adequate stability, we need to see more consistent production from the bottom of the order to avoid further benching of depth pieces like **Kyle Karros** and **Dylan Beavers**. Keep a close eye on the weather and late-inning lineup shifts for **Justin Crawford** and **Daulton Varsho**, as any defensive replacements 